  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back to confirm.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" covers the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`, and its time field is `timestamp`. The fields used below are `Carrier` (string) and `Cancelled` (boolean).\n2. **Dashboard:** I created \"S4-1b2dd2a6 Flights\" ([link](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/s4-1b2dd2a6-flights-a3ae29368565)) with exactly two panels:\n   - **Flights over time:** a line chart of flight count over `timestamp`.\n   - **Carrier panel:** a bar chart of flight count for the top 10 carriers.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created \"S4-1b2dd2a6 Cancelled flights\" as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute, matches `Cancelled: true` and fires when more than 10 cancelled flights fall within 5 minutes. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it separately.\n4. **Edits and check:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\", and the dashboard still has exactly two panels. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
